# Cache policy for the deployed origin. Cloudflare reads this from the build output;
# Vite copies everything in `public/` into `dist/`, which is why it lives here rather
# than beside `wrangler.jsonc`.
#
# The split below is the whole file. Fingerprinted assets never change under their
# name, so they are cached for a year and never revalidated. Everything that names
# them — the shell, the worker, the manifest — must be revalidated on every visit,
# because a returning visitor holding a stale `index.html` is holding script tags that
# point at bundles the last deploy deleted, and a stale `sw.js` registration points at
# a worker that no longer exists. There is no user action that recovers from that: the
# cache keeps handing back the same broken shell. It also cannot happen locally — it
# needs a SECOND deploy to exist, which is why `tests/build/deploy-headers.test.ts`
# asserts both directions rather than trusting a smoke test.
#
# `no-cache` means "revalidate before reuse", NOT "do not store". `no-store` would look
# like the more cautious choice and would defeat the offline cache the service worker
# exists to fill.

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Every client-side route, not just `/`.
#
# A navigation asks for the URL, not for the file — the same reason `vite-plugin-sw.ts`
# precaches `/` by hand rather than relying on `/index.html`. The edge rewrites all of
# these to the same `index.html` bytes (wrangler.jsonc's single-page-application
# handling), so they are all the shell and all must revalidate; a rule that named only
# `/` would leave the four addresses this app just became able to SHARE governed by a
# platform default rather than by this file. Someone who bookmarked `/play` is exactly
# the visitor a stale shell strands.
#
# There is no catch-all here on purpose: `_headers` applies EVERY matching rule, so a
# `/*` block would also match `/assets/*` and send two conflicting Cache-Control values.
# `tests/build/deploy-headers.test.ts` derives this list from the router, so a route
# added without an entry here fails the build tests rather than shipping uncovered.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/lobby
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/play
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/dex
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/edit
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
